const faqs = [
  { q: 'Is Nyungwe chimpanzee trekking as good as Kibale in Uganda?', a: 'Both are excellent chimp trekking destinations with different characters. Kibale in Uganda has more habituated communities and a higher success rate for longer encounters. Nyungwe\'s forest is higher altitude and more challenging, but the combination of chimps, the enormous colobus troops and the Canopy Walk makes it unique. For a Rwanda-only trip, Nyungwe is the best chimpanzee option.' },
  { q: 'Can I combine Nyungwe with Volcanoes National Park on the same trip?', a: 'Yes. Most Rwanda itineraries combine both parks - typically 2 nights at Nyungwe for chimps and the Canopy Walk, then drive north to the Musanze area for 2 to 3 nights of gorilla trekking at Volcanoes. The drive between the two parks takes approximately 5 to 6 hours via Kigali or the southern route via Lake Kivu.' },
  { q: 'What is the Canopy Walk experience like?', a: 'The Nyungwe Canopy Walk consists of a series of steel suspension bridges suspended 50 to 70 metres above the forest floor, connecting observation platforms in the upper canopy. The walk takes approximately 1.5 to 2 hours. It is not physically demanding but can feel vertiginous for those uncomfortable with heights. The perspective from the bridges - looking down into the canopy with colobus monkeys moving through the branches below - is extraordinary.' },
